Title:
NocturneLdr (Nocturne) — CET-Compatible Windows x64 Shellcode Loader with Fully Backed Call Stacks

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Nocturne is a research-focused Windows x64 shellcode loader designed to generate “clean” call stacks that appear fully legitimate during stack walking, including under Intel CET shadow stack validation. It targets a key EDR/forensics signal: anomalous or unbacked return addresses and unwind artifacts that reveal injected code. Instead of fabricating frames, it injects code into a signed module’s `.text` slack space (e.g., `windows.storage.dll`) and registers a `RUNTIME_FUNCTION` using real donor unwind metadata so the Windows unwinder produces believable frames. The project implements BYOUD-style runtime function table manipulation, inverted function table interference, cache invalidation, and WinDbg-oriented hiding (type patching and `.pdata` suppression). It also includes sleep obfuscation (“Zilean”) that encrypts the loader image in memory via a ROP/NtContinue chain, plus stack duplication so `GetThreadContext` sees a benign thread context while sleeping. Useful for red teamers and malware researchers studying modern stack-based detections, CET constraints, and stealthy loader engineering.

Technical Focus:
- CET-aware call stack spoofing / shadow stack compatibility
- BYOUD (Bring Your Own Unwind Data) via `RtlAddFunctionTable` and donor unwind metadata
- Code cave injection into `.text` and unwind chain legitimacy
- `RtlpInvertedFunctionTable` manipulation + unwind cache invalidation
- PEB-walking, compile-time DJB2 API hashing, and IAT camouflage
- Sleep obfuscation: in-memory encryption (SystemFunction040/041), ROP with `NtContinue`, stack duplication, heap masking, EAF bypass (gadget-based reads)

Use Cases:
- Building/experimenting with CET-compatible loaders that survive stack-based EDR heuristics
- Researching Windows x64 unwind internals and runtime function table abuse
- Evaluating defender visibility in WinDbg/Process Hacker stack traces under spoofing
- Developing stealthy sleep routines (image encryption + context spoofing) for long-lived payloads
- Testing EAF-style export-table monitoring bypass techniques

Keywords:
Windows x64, Intel CET, shadow stack, stack spoofing, call stack integrity, BYOUD, unwind info, RUNTIME_FUNCTION, RtlAddFunctionTable, RtlpInvertedFunctionTable, WinDbg bypass, .pdata, code cave injection, PEB walking, DJB2 hashing, API hashing, IAT camouflage, EDR evasion, sleep obfuscation, NtContinue, ROP chain, RtlRegisterWait, SystemFunction040, SystemFunction041, RtlEncryptMemory, heap masking, Export Address Filtering, EAF bypass, windows.storage.dll